
# --- Throughput fix + DPI-evasion for the wg0 tunnel ---
# Spliced into /etc/ufw/before.rules as its own *mangle table block
# (separate from the *filter block in tor_torrent_block.rules).
*mangle
:FORWARD ACCEPT [0:0]
:OUTPUT ACCEPT [0:0]

# Clamp TCP MSS to path MTU for anything crossing wg0 in either direction.
# Without this, a TCP handshake negotiates MSS off each endpoint's own
# interface MTU (usually 1500), oblivious to the tunnel's real 1420 MTU in
# between — the resulting oversized segments then depend on Path MTU
# Discovery (ICMP "fragmentation needed") to get corrected, and that ICMP
# is commonly filtered somewhere on the path. The result is exactly what
# asymmetric-throughput symptoms look like: one direction's bulk-data
# segments black-hole while small requests/acks in the other direction
# keep working fine. --clamp-mss-to-pmtu computes the right value from each
# outgoing interface's actual MTU rather than a hardcoded guess.
-A FORWARD -o wg0 -p tcp -m tcp --tcp-flags SYN,RST SYN -j TCPMSS --clamp-mss-to-pmtu
-A FORWARD -i wg0 -p tcp -m tcp --tcp-flags SYN,RST SYN -j TCPMSS --clamp-mss-to-pmtu

# Normalize the TTL of outgoing WireGuard packets (the encrypted UDP
# envelope itself, generated fresh by the kernel on this host — that is
# an OUTPUT-chain packet, not a FORWARD one, since encryption/encapsulation
# happens locally rather than merely routing an existing packet through).
# Some ISP-level DPI fingerprints VPN traffic partly by an anomalous TTL
# (e.g. one that betrays an extra hop of internal routing/NAT before it
# reaches the wire) rather than by port or payload alone. Port 443 alone
# (see WG_LISTEN_PORT in vpn_node/app/config.py) already blends in with
# HTTPS/QUIC; a consistent, ordinary-looking TTL64 (the common Linux/Android
# default) removes one more heuristic signal. Update --sport if
# WG_LISTEN_PORT is changed again.
-A OUTPUT -p udp --sport 443 -j TTL --ttl-set 64
COMMIT
